      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** Molecular alterations of APP in AD studies are associated mainly with early-onset/autosomal-dominant familial AD (FAD/ADAD), altered Aβ production and aggregation, Down syndrome–related AD, cerebral amyloid angiopathy, neuroinflammatory/microglial phenotypes, tauopathy, synaptic and behavioral deficits, and vascular/metabolic changes.\n\n**Supporting points**\n- **Early-onset familial AD:** Dominant APP mutations cause early-onset FAD, often by altering processive C99 cleavage and Aβ peptide production (PMID 41525885; PMID 42381348). APP/PSEN mutations cause ADAD with near-full penetrance and predictable onset (PMID 41709913).\n- **Aβ production/aggregation:** Variants within or around the Aβ domain mostly increase Aβ production or aggregation (PMID 41592617). The APP E590D mutation increases Aβ and Aη peptides and exacerbates tauopathy (PMID 41816611).\n- **Down syndrome AD:** An extra copy of chromosome 21, containing APP, causes a genetic form of AD (PMID 42230953).\n- **Cerebral amyloid angiopathy:** The Dutch APP mutation (c.2077G>C) causes Dutch-type CAA (PMID 36733499); vasculotropic Dutch-mutant Aβ promotes endothelial apoptosis, barrier permeability, and angiogenic impairment (PMID 41827857).\n- **Neuroinflammation/microglia:** AD-associated APP mutations potentiate Hv1 activity, linking genetic risk to microglial dysfunction (PMID 41780099); E682K and D694N increase C99 voltage-dependent channel activity, rationalizing enhanced neuroinflammation (PMID 41129234).\n- **Tauopathy:** APP E590D increases Aβ/Aη and exacerbates tauopathy (PMID 41816611).\n- **Synaptic/behavioral and vascular/metabolic phenotypes:** In APP/PS1 mice, Nlrp3/Casp1 ablation rescued spatial memory, LTP, and spine loss (PMID 38017562); APP Val717Ile was linked to thalamic hypometabolism in an EOAD case (PMID 21559247); APP/PS1 mice show infection-induced BBB breakdown (PMID 34764472).\n\n**Limitations**\nMany phenotypes derive from transgenic/knock-in mice or cell models (e.g., PMID 38017562; PMID 42039580; PMID 42123478), so they show association/mechanism, not proven causation in humans. Some records are preprints or lack clear phenotype specificity (e.g., PMID 40654986; PMID 42129847). Evidence for direct APP mutation effects on mitochondrial, cholinergic, and oxidative phenotypes is mixed or indirect (PMID 34867159; PMID 14522000; PMID 32486013)."
