      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In AD-Alterome–curated sentences, molecular alterations of APP (mostly FAD point mutations, plus promoter hypomethylation and transgenic overexpression) are associated with amyloid/Abeta phenotypes, mitochondrial and lysosomal dysfunction, synaptic and calcium abnormalities, neuroinflammatory/BBB changes, and structural brain changes. Most evidence is from cell or mouse models; human data are limited.\n\n**Amyloid and metabolic phenotypes**\n- APP promoter hypomethylation is reported to cause APP overexpression and overproduction/abnormal Abeta metabolism (PMID 36841702).\n- FAD APP mutations in patient-derived neural progenitors replicate increased Abeta42 secretion and elevated Abeta42/40 ratios (PMID 37901842).\n- In familial AD due to APP/PSEN mutations, beta-amyloid accumulation in senile plaques and amyloid angiopathy is described as the primary defect, with hyperphosphorylated tau pathology linked to clinical phenotype (PMID 26550830).\n\n**Mitochondrial, lysosomal, and autophagic phenotypes**\n- Overexpression of AD-associated mutant APP led to abnormal mitochondrial biogenesis/mitophagy and mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 33080054).\n- In AD brains and mutant APP models, Fis1 expression and DRP1 GTPase activity are increased (PMID 34867159).\n- An APP mutation (E693Q) mouse showed intraneuronal lysosomal APP-CTF/oligomeric Abeta accumulation, implicating lysosomal dysfunction (PMID 35847516).\n- APP/PS1 mice lacking NRF2 showed autophagy dysfunction–dependent insoluble Abeta accumulation and an increased pro-inflammatory phenotype (PMID 37840813).\n\n**Synaptic, calcium, and structural phenotypes**\n- FAD mutants of PS and APP alter calcium homeostasis and cause synaptic dysfunction and dendritic spine loss in neurons/mice (PMID 22453989).\n- APP FAD mutations produce varying pathological phenotypes across mouse models and patients; APP Swedish mutation carriers show atrophy with sulcal widening and mild ventricular enlargement (PMID 37259128).\n\n**Neuroinflammation/BBB and cellular phenotypes**\n- Aged APP/PS1 mice challenged with endotoxin or live bacteria showed more robust Il1b upregulation and BBB breakdown (fibrinogen deposition, monocyte/T-cell infiltration) (PMID 34764472).\n- 5XFAD mice overexpressing human APP with three FAD mutations showed increased neuronal p16 senescence, but not in astrocytes or microglia (PMID 35216123).\n\n**Other reported associations**\n- Enhancing myelin renewal rescued cognition and hippocampal physiology deficits in APP/PS1 mice (PMID 36605616).\n- AD-associated knock-in App mutations altered App-EV abundance and App metabolite trafficking (PMID 42129847).\n- 4-PBA is reported to enhance trafficking of APP mutants via heat shock protein effects (PMID 28025703).\n\n**Limitations:** These are sentence-level associations from a bounded AD-Alterome candidate pool, not proof of causation. Many derive from transgenic/overexpression or mutant models rather than human tissue; direction, model, and intervention are sometimes unresolved in the source sentences. Conflicting or model-specific findings should be preserved rather than merged into a single mechanism."
