      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** APOE-related molecular evidence most strongly implicates the **amyloid**, **tau**, **neuroinflammation**, and **vascular** hypotheses, with additional support for **mitochondrial/autophagy**, **oxidative stress**, **lipid/metabolic**, and **metal-ion** mechanisms. Evidence for the **microbiota–gut–brain axis** and **cholinergic** hypotheses is weaker or indirect.\n\n**Supporting points**\n- **Amyloid hypothesis:** APOE4 impairs Aβ clearance via disrupted meningeal lymphatic function (PMID 41152198); APOE4 reduces Aβ clearance and enhances tau pathology (PMID 40943807); APOE4 alters TREM2–apoE binding (PMID 41085188); brain cholesterol transport defects link ApoE4 to AD pathology (PMID 40164234).\n- **Tau hypothesis:** APOE4 enhances tau pathology (PMID 40943807); ApoE3 R136S reduces tau uptake into neurons/microglia and tau fragmentation (PMID 39814008); APOE rs769449 associates with higher plasma p-tau181 (PMID 35250029); APOE ε4 interacts with tau haplotypes (PMID 11303757).\n- **Neuroinflammation:** APOE4 and TREM2 variants compromise mitochondrial integrity and lipid metabolism, sensitizing innate surveillance failure (PMID 42071158); APOE4 affects microglial lipid handling (PMID 42194007); ApoE4 links to increased neuroinflammation and lipid-droplet accumulation (PMID 40451545); APOE4 elevates ceramide synthesis (PMID 40295359).\n- **Vascular hypothesis:** APOE ε4 disrupts meningeal lymphatics and Aβ clearance (PMID 41152198); ε4 homozygosity exacerbates retinal/cerebral microvascular dysfunction mediating cognitive impairment (PMID 41395713); ε4 allele number associates with white-matter hyperintensities and cerebrovascular reactivity (PMID 17545732); APOE4 iPSCs show reduced lymphatic marker genes (PMID 35410605, 32355332).\n- **Mitochondrial/autophagy & oxidative stress:** APOE4 compromises mitochondrial integrity (PMID 42071158); APOE4 fibroblasts show autophagy, mitochondrial fragmentation, superoxide, and lysosome changes (PMID 37455931); apoE4 promotes GRP75, mitochondrial dysfunction, and calcium transport (PMID 33676568).\n- **Metal-ion hypothesis:** apoE4 arginine substitutions limit zinc homeostasis control (PMID 31991844).\n- **Microbiota–gut–brain axis:** APOE4 affects microglial lipid handling alongside gut microbiota (PMID 42194007); APOE4 associates with gut microbiome profiles (PMID 38369241); germ-free ApoE4 mice show reduced p-tau and hippocampal atrophy (PMID 38964948).\n- **Cholinergic hypothesis:** indirect—wild-type MGE transplants improve behavior in apoE4-KI mice (PMID 29551491).\n\n**Limitations**\nMost mechanistic evidence comes from cell or animal models (e.g., iPSC astrocytes, knock-in mice), not human causal proof. Several records are preprints or lack full identifiers. Associations (e.g., p-tau181, WMH) do not establish causation. Hypotheses overlap, and the database does not quantify relative contributions."
