      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** APOE-related molecular evidence in this AD-Alterome packet most strongly implicates the **amyloid hypothesis**, with recurring links to **tau, neuroinflammation, vascular/lipid, and autophagy-lysosomal hypotheses**. Most records are associations or readouts, not causal demonstrations.\n\n**Amyloid hypothesis.** APOE4 is reported to increase propensity for Aβ aggregation in sporadic AD and Aβ deposition in capillaries (PMID 24587158). APOE is described as implicated in brain cholesterol and amyloid metabolism, with APOE-e4 a major predisposing AD factor (PMID 21760717). APOE-e4, with other variants, was associated with Aβ plaque score and neuritic plaque score in Han Chinese (PMID 34050523). A sentence also lists APOE mutations among genes contributing to Aβ oligomer toxicity and deposition, \"causing familial AD\" (PMID 35794091) — but this grouping is broad and the familial attribution is not APOE-specific.\n\n**Tau hypothesis.** APOE-e4 carriers showed altered IGF-II receptor levels in affected AD brain regions alongside tau-positive tangles (PMID 24846272); APOE-e4 was associated with Braak tangle stage (PMID 34050523). In APOE4/APOE3-4 patient organoids with PSEN1 M146V, W2A-28 treatment suppressed tau phosphorylation (PMID 42146614) — a drug effect in a multi-mutation model, not APOE perturbation alone.\n\n**Neuroinflammation.** IL-1A polymorphisms influenced microglial activation, especially in APOE-e4 carriers, supporting neuroinflammation in AD pathogenesis (PMID 15377701). APOE is listed among AD risk variants associated with microglial dysfunction and chronic inflammation (PMID 33814444).\n\n**Vascular/lipid.** Cholesterol metabolism and efflux pathways were implicated in AD pathogenesis independently of apoE isoforms (PMID 32065798); APOE polymorphism is associated with vascular disease and AD (PMID 34425108; PMID 36077289).\n\n**Autophagy/lysosomal.** The effect of apoE4 on lysosomal homeostasis is explicitly stated as incompletely explored (PMID 37873080).\n\n**Limitations.** These are sentence-level, lexically screened records; several are reviews, hypotheses, or associations. Direction, model, and causality are often unresolved, and annotation-derived hypothesis labels are retrieval aids, not proof. Coverage is bounded to this database and does not establish a complete literature picture."
