      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** AD-Alterome curation links MAPT/tau to several molecular pathological alterations in AD-related studies: tau hyperphosphorylation, tau aggregation into neurofibrillary tangles, altered 3R/4R splicing, and specific MAPT mutations/variants associated with tau pathology or AD-related phenotypes. Most evidence is associative or model-based rather than causal in human AD.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- **Tau hyperphosphorylation and aggregation:** Tau aggregation in neurofibrillary tangles is described as a pathological hallmark of tauopathies including AD (PMID 20930284). MAPT-N296H was reported to cause 3R:4R splicing imbalance and tau hyperphosphorylation (PMID 28233851). In hTau mice, CX3CR1 deficiency enhanced tau phosphorylation, aggregation, and memory impairment (PMID 37735487).\n- **Splicing/isoform changes:** Differential 3R/4R MAPT splice-variant transcriptional profiles are linked to tau aggregation (PMID 20930284); MAPT-N296H disrupts the 3R:4R ratio (PMID 28233851).\n- **Specific MAPT variants:** MAPT V337M was reported with increased tau phosphorylation, glutamatergic dysfunction, and neuron loss in human cortical organoids (PMID 35985329). MAPT-A152T drove neuronal hyperactivity via Fyn-NMDAR signaling in human iPSC-derived neurons (PMID 39811068). MAPT R406W produced neurofibrillary tangles ultrastructurally similar to AD (PMID 27357347). A MAPT p.Q124E variant co-occurred with Alzheimer-type tau pathology in one LRRK2 p.G2019S case (PMID 33494262).\n- **Model/context caveats:** 3xTg-AD mice carry MAPT P301L alongside APP/PSEN1 mutations and develop hyperphosphorylated tau aggregates (PMID 34744620; PMID 26697132). MAPT mutations are also implicated in non-AD tauopathies (PMID 36672187), and some sources state MAPT mutations cause related dementias rather than AD itself (PMID 34753904).\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- Several records are `needs_review` or lack explicit AD model/patient context; direction and intervention entities are sometimes unresolved (e.g., PMID 33679286; PMID 35012639).\n- Findings come from mixed models (human post-mortem, iPSC, mouse), so association versus causation and human versus model evidence must be kept separate.\n- This synthesis is based only on the retrieved AD-Alterome records and does not establish full literature coverage or verified causality."
