      "original_en": "MAPT/tau-related alterations in AD center on abnormal tau phosphorylation, aggregation, and downstream neuronal dysfunction, with genetic evidence strongest for causality in frontotemporal dementia (FTD) and model systems rather than sporadic AD.\n\n**Key reported alterations**\n- **Tau aggregation and phosphorylation:** Tau aggregation in AD correlates with neuron loss, brain atrophy, and cognitive decline; pro-aggregation tau mutations can cause neurodegeneration and dementia in humans and tauopathy mice (PMID 41486697). Phosphorylated tau occurs in excitatory and inhibitory neurons in AD and other tauopathies (PMID 40481857).\n- **Axonal transport and morphology:** Aberrant tau accumulation from MAPT mutations induces early axonal-transport pathology, rescued by p38α inhibition (PMID 42243402). The V337M mutation perturbs tau phosphorylation and axon morphology pathways (PMID 42046563; PMID 38895329).\n- **Nucleolar and nuclear stress:** Mutant MAPT induces rDNA transcriptional hyperactivation and nucleolar stress in cellular models (PMID 41255983); pathogenic tau reduces nuclear tension, with elevated emerin in MAPT-mutant iPSC neurons (PMID 41278948).\n- **Lipid and metabolic changes:** The P301S mutation increases diglycerides and hexosyl-/lactosylceramides while reducing triglycerides, before pTau accumulation (PMID 41318029). Mutant tau (P301L) upregulates mTOR signaling and global translation in SH-SY5Y cells (PMID 41516328).\n- **Proteostasis/autophagy:** Tau is among proteins accumulating with UPS dysfunction (PMID 36453392); IKKβ deficiency enhances autophagy in tau-transgenic mice (PMID 36688823).\n- **Biomarker/clinical associations:** TREM2-R47H carriers show higher CSF total tau and p-tau Thr181 (PMID 33255694). Hypertension modulates the relationship between cerebral Aβ and tau deposition (PMID 37626495).\n- **Circuit/behavioral effects:** Mutant tau-P301S in spinal CCK neurons activates c-Maf, inhibiting these neurons and causing tactile deficits; silencing tau or c-Maf restores sensation and improves cognition in AD mice (PMID 41254283).\n\n**Limitations**\nMost mechanistic findings derive from cell or animal models and MAPT mutations linked to FTD/FTDP-17, not sporadic AD; causality in human AD remains uncertain. Some records are preprints (e.g., PMID 41255983, 41278948, 38895329), requiring cautious interpretation."
