      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In AD-related studies, reducing PINK1 expression/function is predominantly harmful—worsening amyloid, tau, Lewy-body, oxidative, inflammatory, and autophagic pathology—whereas enhancing PINK1 is largely protective, restoring mitophagy and reducing protein aggregates and cognitive deficits. However, one study reports an opposite effect on aggregation, and most evidence comes from cell and animal models rather than human AD.\n\n**Reducing PINK1 (loss of function):**\n- PINK1 deficiency synergizes with Aβ to promote Lewy pathology via loss of protective α-synuclein phosphorylation (PMID 40990068).\n- Loss/knockdown of PINK1 produces excessive ROS, increased NFKB1/p50 and RELA/p65, elevated BACE1 and γ-secretase, and Aβ accumulation (PMID 40320714).\n- PINK1 downregulation causes mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, and neuronal dysfunction (PMID 36950017); absence of PINK1 triggers a STING-mediated type I IFN response promoting neurodegeneration (PMID 37175853).\n- PINK1 knockout alters lysosome morphology, impairing autophagic substrate clearance (PMID 30781825), and lack of PINK1 increases glia-mediated neuronal apoptosis (PMID 33168089).\n- Mutant PINK1 is associated with increased tau hyperphosphorylation (PMID 25899925).\n\n**Enhancing PINK1 (gain of function):**\n- PINK1 overexpression promotes tau degradation, rescuing neuronal loss, synaptic damage, and cognitive impairment in a tauopathy mouse model (PMID 36325189).\n- AAV2-hPINK1 delivery in transgenic AD-model rats stimulated mitophagy via NDP52 and OPTN receptors (PMID 37109499).\n- Restoring PINK1-Parkin signaling (e.g., via BBR reversing promoter methylation) improved mitochondrial function, reduced AD pathology burden, and reduced cognitive deficits (PMID 36982968).\n\n**Conflicting/limiting evidence:**\n- One study found Pink1 knockout *mitigated* protein aggregation in mouse brains and HEK293 cells (PMID 40742280), contradicting the protective-loss pattern.\n- Many records concern Parkinson's disease or general neurodegeneration rather than AD specifically (e.g., PMIDs 38004442, 37631265, 37377861), so extrapolation to AD is indirect.\n- Most findings are from cell lines or animal models; human causal evidence in AD remains limited. Directionality (association vs. causation) is therefore uncertain, and the net effect of PINK1 modulation in human AD is not established."
