      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In the retrieved AD-Alterome evidence, reducing PINK1 is consistently harmful—it impairs mitophagy, increases amyloid/tau pathology, oxidative stress, and neuronal dysfunction—whereas enhancing PINK1 is largely protective, promoting mitophagy, clearing Aβ/tau, and rescuing synaptic and cognitive deficits. Most support comes from cell and animal models, not human AD tissue.\n\n**Reducing PINK1 (loss/knockdown):**\n- In APP transgenic mice, PINK1 knockdown impaired mitophagy and increased plaque deposition (PMID 32339611).\n- Loss or knockdown of PINK1 produced excessive ROS, increased BACE1/γ-secretase components, and Aβ accumulation (PMID 40320714).\n- PINK1 downregulation led to mitochondrial dysfunction, increased oxidative stress, and neuronal dysfunction; PINK1 is associated with AD onset/progression (PMID 36950017).\n- In non-AD mammalian cells, PINK1 knockdown caused mitochondrial fragmentation with enhanced autophagic turnover—an effect opposite to the AD-model direction, and the sentence does not establish an AD context (PMID 19799998).\n\n**Enhancing PINK1 (overexpression):**\n- In APP transgenic mice, PINK1 overexpression enhanced mitophagy and reduced plaque deposition (PMID 32339611).\n- In mAPP mice, PINK1 overexpression increased LC3-II, LAMP1, cathepsin D, OPTN, and NDP52, leading to Aβ plaque clearance (PMID 35059394).\n- PINK1 overexpression promoted tau degradation and rescued neuronal loss, synaptic damage, and cognitive impairment in a tauopathy mouse model (PMID 36325189).\n- PINK1 overexpression activated Nrf2 signaling, increasing antioxidant proteins and reducing oxidative damage (PMID 34893682).\n\n**Interpretation and limitations:**\n- Directionality is fairly consistent across these model studies: loss is deleterious, gain is protective, plausibly via mitophagy and oxidative-stress pathways. However, these are associations/interventions in model systems; causation in human AD is not established.\n- Several sentences lack explicit AD context (e.g., PMIDs 19799998, 34893682), and some are `needs_review` cards; their AD relevance is inferred from database labels, not the sentence itself.\n- Conflicting/uncertain findings exist: PINK1 knockdown enhanced autophagy in non-AD cells (PMID 19799998), and PINK1 mutations are primarily linked to Parkinson's disease rather than AD (PMID 26903445).\n- No human AD patient evidence on PINK1 manipulation was retrieved; conclusions should be limited to preclinical models."
