      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** The AD-Alterome packet contains only a few sources that explicitly name an experimental model while linking PPARGC1A (PGC-1α) to mitochondrial abnormalities in an AD context. The clearest are: (1) a hippocampus/cortex-restricted PGC-1α knockout mouse line (Dlx5/6-Cre:PGC-1α^fl/fl), and (2) neuroblastoma N2a cells expressing mutant APP. Most other records are human genetic/expression studies or non-AD mechanistic statements and do not establish an AD experimental model.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- **Mouse (conditional PGC-1α deficiency):** A specific mouse line, Dlx5/6-Cre:PGC-1α^fl/fl, with PGC-1α deficiency limited to hippocampus and cortex, showed decreased VDR and increased oxidative damage in AD-related brain regions (PMID 31410709? no — PMID 33373677). This is an in vivo model directly manipulating PGC-1α in AD-relevant regions.\n- **Cell model (mutant APP neuroblastoma):** In neuroblastoma N2a cells grown with mutant APP, treatment with SS-31 prevented mitochondrial dysfunction, enhanced ΔΨm, and increased the neuroprotective gene PGC-1α, suggesting beneficial effects on mitochondrial alterations in AD (PMID 33679375). Here PGC-1α is a readout, not the perturbed target.\n- **Transgenic APP mouse (overexpression):** PGC-1α overexpression exacerbated neuropathological and behavioral deficits in transgenic mice with APP mutations associated with human AD (PMID 24398293). This links PGC-1α manipulation to AD-like pathology in vivo, though the sentence emphasizes neuropathology/behavior rather than mitochondrial readouts specifically.\n- **Human tissue/patient evidence (not a model):** PGC-1α abundance was decreased in AD, HD, and PD patients (PMID 25118933); dysregulated PGC1α has been implicated in neurodegenerative disorders such as AD (PMID 25961951). These are human association findings, not experimental models.\n- **Non-AD or unspecified models:** Several records describe PGC-1α effects on mitochondrial biogenesis/dynamics in neurons, skeletal muscle cells, or unspecified systems (e.g., PMIDs 30081787, 34836000, 25206336, 38424050, 31614723) without an AD model context; they should not be counted as AD-model evidence.\n\n**Limitations and uncertainty**\n\n- The packet is small (18 selected sentences) and most rows are flagged `needs_review`; sentence-level screening is lexical triage, not expert validation.\n- Several sentences mention AD only as a label or in a mixed-disease list (e.g., PMID 31410709), so they do not establish a dedicated AD experiment.\n- Directionality is inconsistent: PGC-1α overexpression is reported as both protective (mitochondrial function) and exacerbating (APP mouse pathology), so no single causal direction should be asserted.\n- Human findings are associative; causal claims require the specific perturbation models above, and even those remain model-bound."
