      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** AD-Alterome–curated sentences link mitochondrial dysfunction in AD to alterations in **APP, PSEN1, PSEN2, APOE4, TOMM40, ALDH2, PINK1, CDK5, RCAN1, FGF13, FRMD6, cystatin C, and BACE1**, with additional mentions of **LRRK2, huntingtin, and IL-6** in broader neurodegeneration/inflammation contexts.\n\n**Supporting points (source-bound):**\n\n- **APP, PSEN1, PSEN2, APOE4:** \"AD mutants affecting the presenilins, Aβ, APP, and ApoE4 are associated with mitochondria and cause mitochondrial dysfunction and oxidative damage\" (PMID 36927428). The 5X-FAD mouse (APP + PSEN1 mutations) shows mitochondrial dysfunction with intraneuronal Aβ42 (PMID 33624140). Mutations in APP, PSEN1, PSEN2 are listed among defects promoting AD alongside mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 26811881; PMID 37489441).\n- **TOMM40:** AD-associated mutant TOMM40 (F113L/F131L) expressed in BV2 microglial cells induced mitochondrial dysfunction and oxidative stress (PMID 36835494). TOMM40 polymorphisms are also implicated in AD with mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 27079717).\n- **ALDH2:** ALDH2*2 mutation/overexpression in AD patient fibroblasts increased oxidative stress and mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 31829281); ALDH2 knockout mice with APOE ε4 showed increased oxidative stress and mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 34151801).\n- **PINK1:** PINK1 downregulation leads to mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, and neuronal dysfunction; PINK1 is associated with AD onset/progression (PMID 36950017).\n- **CDK5:** Cdk5 deregulation contributes to AD pathology including mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 26227906).\n- **RCAN1:** RCAN1 overexpression linked to oxidative stress and mitochondrial dysfunction, though in vivo relevance to AD onset was noted as unexplored (PMID 26497675).\n- **FGF13:** FGF13 hypermethylation reduces microtubule stability via TUBB2A, promoting mitochondrial dysfunction in AD (PMID 41808420).\n- **FRMD6 (Willin):** FRMD6 knockdown leads to mitochondrial dysfunction and fragmentation, reported as early AD features (PMID 36231104).\n- **Cystatin C:** Variant B cystatin C interacts with mitochondrial processes, contributing to mitochondrial dysfunction characteristic of AD (PMID 36899848).\n- **BACE1:** Drp1 inhibition restored Aβ-mediated mitochondrial dysfunction and reduced BACE1 expression in AD mice (PMID 30144530) — BACE1 appears as a downstream readout, not the perturbed gene.\n- **LRRK2, huntingtin, IL-6:** Mentioned in sentences about neurodegeneration-associated mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 24413616; PMID 28167533; PMID 27079717), but these are not AD-specific perturbations in the cited sentences.\n\n**Limitations:**\n\n- Most evidence is **association or model-based** (mouse, cell lines, patient fibroblasts), not direct human causal proof.\n- Several sentences are reviews or broad statements; the specific gene–mitochondria causal direction is often unresolved in the source wording.\n- LRRK2, huntingtin, and IL-6 sentences describe non-AD or mixed neurodegeneration contexts; they should not be read as AD-specific mitochondrial gene alterations.\n- This synthesis is based on AD-Alterome curated sentences only; no external literature search was performed, and sentence-level screening is not expert validation."
