      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In AD research, mitochondrial dysfunction has been most consistently linked to **APP**, **PSEN1**, **PSEN2**, **APOE** (especially APOE4), and **MAPT**, with additional associations reported for **PINK1**, **Parkin (PRKN)**, **DJ-1 (PARK7)**, **TFAM**, **POLG**, and some mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) variants/haplogroups. These are associations; causation is established only for rare familial mutations (APP/PSEN1/PSEN2) and is model-dependent for the rest.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- **APP, PSEN1, PSEN2:** Familial AD mutations alter amyloid precursor protein processing and presenilin function. Presenilins also localize to mitochondria-associated membranes and affect calcium handling, ROS, and mitochondrial dynamics. Human mutation carriers and transgenic models show mitochondrial deficits, but the same mutations cause early-onset dementia through multiple pathways, so mitochondrial effects are not the sole cause. (PMID: 12805266; PMID: 17121831)\n- **APOE:** The ε4 allele is the strongest common late-onset AD risk factor. APOE4 is associated with impaired mitochondrial respiration, reduced complex IV activity, altered mitochondrial dynamics, and increased oxidative stress in human brain tissue, iPSC-derived neurons, and mouse models. APOE genotype is a risk modifier, not a Mendelian cause of mitochondrial disease. (PMID: 24445058; PMID: 29346673)\n- **MAPT:** Tau pathology is associated with mitochondrial transport defects, impaired complex I activity, and increased ROS. Most evidence comes from cell and animal models; human causal direction remains uncertain. (PMID: 17553425)\n- **PINK1, PRKN, DJ-1:** These Parkinson's disease–related genes regulate mitophagy and mitochondrial quality control. They are not classic AD genes, but AD brains and models show altered mitophagy, and variants have been studied as modifiers. Evidence for a direct AD-specific role is limited. (PMID: 20519542)\n- **TFAM, POLG, mtDNA:** TFAM maintains mtDNA; POLG replicates it. Variants and reduced expression are reported in AD and in models with mtDNA depletion, oxidative stress, and respiratory chain defects. mtDNA haplogroups and somatic mutations have shown inconsistent associations across cohorts. (PMID: 17409317; PMID: 22423891)\n\n**Limitations and uncertainty**\n\nMost mitochondrial gene associations in AD are correlative, derived from postmortem tissue, cell models, or animals, and are confounded by age, brain region, and comorbid pathology. Rare familial AD mutations provide stronger causal evidence for mitochondrial involvement, but even there mitochondria are one of several affected systems. Common variants in PINK1, PRKN, TFAM, POLG, and mtDNA have not been consistently replicated as AD risk factors. No single \"mitochondrial AD gene\" is established. Reliable source identifiers for some specific claims could not be verified in this session, so those statements are presented as general findings rather than tied to a single traceable record."
