      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In AD studies, oxidative stress is linked mainly to oxidative damage to lipids, proteins, DNA/RNA, altered antioxidant defenses, mitochondrial dysfunction, and metal/redox imbalance. These are largely associative and often model-dependent.\n\n**Key molecular alterations**\n\n- **Lipid peroxidation:** Increased malondialdehyde (MDA), 4-hydroxynonenal (4-HNE), F2-isoprostanes, and acrolein in AD brain, CSF, and plasma; 4-HNE can modify proteins and promote amyloid-beta (Aβ) aggregation. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n- **Protein oxidation:** Elevated protein carbonyls, nitrated proteins (3-nitrotyrosine), and oxidized/nitrated Aβ and tau; nitration may affect tau filament assembly. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n- **DNA/RNA oxidation:** Increased 8-hydroxy-2′-deoxyguanosine (8-OHdG) and 8-hydroxyguanosine in AD brain and CSF, especially in vulnerable regions. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n- **Antioxidant system changes:** Reduced glutathione, altered superoxide dismutase (SOD1/2), catalase, glutathione peroxidase, and lower ascorbate/α-tocopherol in affected brain regions; findings vary by region and stage. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n- **Mitochondrial dysfunction:** Decreased complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase) activity, increased reactive oxygen species (ROS) production, and mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) mutations/oxidation in AD brain and peripheral cells. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n- **Metal/redox imbalance:** Altered iron, copper, and zinc homeostasis; Aβ–metal interactions can generate ROS, and iron accumulation is associated with oxidative damage. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n- **Advanced glycation/lipoxidation end products:** Increased AGEs, pentosidine, and carboxymethyllysine in AD brain, linking oxidative and glycoxidative stress. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n\n**Interpretation and limitations**\n\nMost human evidence is cross-sectional, so it shows association, not causation. Cell and animal models show Aβ and tau can induce oxidative stress and that oxidative modifications can reciprocally promote aggregation, suggesting a feed-forward loop, but this does not prove oxidative stress initiates AD. Some antioxidant biomarkers differ between brain, CSF, and blood, and clinical trials of antioxidants (e.g., vitamin E) have shown limited or no clear benefit, so the causal role remains uncertain. (PMID: 11369029; PMID: 15068966)\n\n**References**\n\n- Markesbery WR. Oxidative stress hypothesis in Alzheimer's disease. Free Radic Biol Med. 1997. PMID: 11369029.\n- Butterfield DA, et al. Evidence of oxidative damage in Alzheimer's disease brain. Trends Mol Med. 2002. PMID: 15068966.\n\n*Note: The PMIDs above are cited from memory and may not map exactly to the intended articles; they should be verified before formal use.*"
