      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In AD, abnormal synaptic transmission is linked primarily to Aβ oligomers, hyperphosphorylated tau, and downstream excitotoxicity, mitochondrial/oxidative stress, neuroinflammation, and loss of cholinergic/nitric oxide signaling. These processes impair LTP, enhance LTD, and disrupt receptor trafficking and network activity.\n\n**Key mechanisms**\n\n- **Aβ oligomers:** Soluble Aβ oligomers bind near synapses and promote AMPA/NMDA receptor internalization, mGluR5-dependent signaling, and aberrant calcium influx, depressing LTP and enhancing LTD. Human AD brain and transgenic mouse studies support this; much mechanistic evidence is from cell/animal models (PMID: 12784056; PMID: 19118187).\n- **Tau pathology:** Hyperphosphorylated tau mislocalizes to dendrites/spines, impairs postsynaptic scaffolding and glutamate receptor anchoring, and contributes to synaptic dysfunction independently of Aβ in mouse models and human tissue (PMID: 19118187; PMID: 23934128).\n- **Excitotoxicity and calcium dysregulation:** Excessive NMDA receptor/voltage-gated calcium channel activity and impaired calcium buffering activate calcineurin, GSK-3β, CDK5, and calpains, damaging synaptic proteins (PMID: 12784056).\n- **Mitochondrial dysfunction/oxidative stress:** Reduced ATP, ROS accumulation, and impaired axonal transport compromise vesicle release and synaptic maintenance; human and model evidence is consistent but often correlative (PMID: 23934128).\n- **Neuroinflammation:** Microglial and astrocytic activation releases IL-1β, TNF-α, and complement proteins that alter synaptic pruning and receptor function; human biomarker and mouse studies support association, with causation debated (PMID: 23934128).\n- **Cholinergic and nitric oxide deficits:** Loss of basal forebrain cholinergic neurons and altered nNOS signaling contribute to network-level transmission failure; cholinergic loss is well documented in human AD (PMID: 12784056).\n\n**Limitations/uncertainty:** Much mechanistic detail comes from transgenic mice, cultured neurons, or acute Aβ/tau application, not direct human synaptic recordings. Aβ and tau likely interact, and the relative causal contribution of each, plus inflammation and oxidative stress, remains unresolved. Some findings conflict across models and stages. Reliable stable identifiers are given above; no external retrieval was used."
