      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** In AD-related studies, abnormal synaptic transmission is linked mainly to glutamatergic excitotoxicity, amyloid/tau pathology, neuroinflammation, impaired autophagy/mitochondrial function, and cholinergic/neurotrophic signaling deficits. Most mechanistic evidence comes from cell and animal models; human evidence is largely genetic/association-based.\n\n**Supporting points**\n- **Glutamatergic excitotoxicity:** Amyloid-beta and hyperphosphorylated tau are reported to be particularly neurotoxic to glutamatergic neurotransmission (PMID 34130857). Familial AD genes APP and PSEN1/PSEN2 are implicated in glutamatergic transmission altered by pathogenic mutations (PMID 33172889). APP loss increases excitatory transmission in animal studies (PMID 33279949); APLP1 deletion enhances excitatory transmission (PMID 25728909); BACE1 knockout strengthens basal excitatory transmission (PMID 24963413).\n- **Amyloid pathway:** APP/PS1 transgenic mice show altered synaptic transmission and cognition (PMID 22779770); PSEN mutations, plaques, and ApoE4 are tied to calcium dyshomeostasis needed for normal transmission (PMID 31366155).\n- **Tau:** The human Tau-A152T mutation enhanced hippocampal CA3 synaptic transmission and epileptiform susceptibility, apparently via delayed proteasomal tau clearance (PMID 29621183).\n- **Neuroinflammation:** TREM2 overexpression raised synaptic proteins PSD95, spinophilin, synaptophysin (PMID 35224156); reactive astrocytes increased basal CA1 transmission via presynaptic adenosine A2A receptors (PMID 24456263); JAK2/STAT3 activation altered transmission (PMID 33339351).\n- **Autophagy/mitochondria:** IF1 ablation impaired memory while overexpression enhanced transmission and learning (PMID 33983919); miRNA loss (Dicer/DGCR8) altered synaptic proteins and transmission (PMID 28851397); miR-128 inhibition of STIM2 caused early mossy-cell synaptic degeneration (PMID 32222058).\n- **Cholinergic/neurotrophic:** TRKA agonism enhanced cholinergic neurotransmission in APP-mutant mice (PMID 36170028); NGF-NTRK1-TRIO variants may regulate transmission (PMID 27908981).\n\n**Limitations:** These are predominantly mouse/cell findings; causality in human AD is not established. Several records are associative (e.g., TMEM106B networks, PMID 36186441) or derive from non-AD models, so conclusions about human synaptic failure remain tentative."
