      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** The AD hypotheses linked to molecular evidence on mitochondrial fission are chiefly the **Mitochondrial Autophagy (mitochondrial dysfunction/mitophagy) Hypothesis**, with secondary links to the **Amyloid Hypothesis**, the **Tau Protein Hypothesis**, and the **Oxidative Stress Hypothesis**.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- *Mitochondrial Autophagy Hypothesis (dominant link).* Most retrieved records assign mitochondrial fission to this hypothesis. In AD brains and mutant APP models, fission proteins Fis1 and DRP1 activity are increased (PMID 34867159); mutant APP raises DRP1/FIS1 and lowers fusion and mitophagy markers (PINK1) in HT22 neurons (PMID 32679689). Aged mutant APP mice show altered fission/fusion proteins plus reduced autophagy/mitophagy markers (PMID 35163247). Mechanistic work implicates DRP1 activation, excessive fragmentation, and impaired mitochondrial transport (PMID 21145355), and reduced CEND1 with Drp1 upregulation in AD pathogenesis (PMID 37159984, 35732922).\n\n- *Amyloid Hypothesis.* Mutant APP/amyloid-beta precursor protein drives fission-fusion imbalance (PMID 35163247, 32679689, 36203194), and BACE1 (amyloidogenic pathway) inhibition alters fission/fusion and mitophagy proteins (PMID 34679674, 37016576).\n\n- *Tau Protein Hypothesis.* Hyperphosphorylated tau interacts abnormally with Drp1, causing excessive fission and synaptic degeneration in APP, APP/PS1, and 3xTg-AD mice (PMID 29623026; also PMID 36408110).\n\n- *Oxidative Stress Hypothesis.* Fission is co-annotated with oxidative stress via SIRT1/Pin1-related ROS and fission markers (PMID 31614723) and PARP-1 modulation of fission/fusion genes (PMID 29128369).\n\n**Limitations and interpretation**\n\n- These are largely **associations**; most evidence comes from **cell and animal models** (mutant APP mice, HT22 neurons), not direct human causal proof. Human post-mortem findings (PMID 34867159) support association but not causation.\n- Several records concern non-AD proteins (HTT, SOD1, VCP, LRRK2, PGC-1alpha) and are annotated \"not applicable to any AD hypothesis\"; they should not be used as AD-specific evidence.\n- Hypothesis labels are database annotations and may overlap; the same gene can map to multiple hypotheses, so the fission–hypothesis link is strongest for mitochondrial autophagy/dynamics and weaker, more indirect, for amyloid, tau, and oxidative stress.\n- No single hypothesis is proven causal; mitochondrial fission is best viewed as a convergent downstream mechanism."
