      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** Mitochondrial fission is linked mainly to the **mitochondrial cascade/dysfunction hypothesis** and the **amyloid-beta (Aβ) cascade hypothesis**, with secondary links to **tau/neurodegeneration** and **oxidative stress** hypotheses. The strongest molecular evidence comes from cell and animal models showing that Aβ and tau promote **Drp1-mediated fission** (often via S-nitrosylation or phosphorylation), while **Mfn1/2 and Opa1** (fusion) are reduced.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- **Aβ → Drp1 activation/fission:** Aβ oligomers increase mitochondrial fragmentation and Drp1 activity; inhibiting Drp1 (e.g., Mdivi-1 or Drp1 knockdown) reduces Aβ-induced mitochondrial and synaptic toxicity in cultured neurons and AD-model mice. This supports the amyloid cascade hypothesis, but most evidence is from models, not direct human causation. (e.g., PMID: 18230636; PMID: 20080728)\n- **Mitochondrial cascade hypothesis:** Abnormal mitochondrial dynamics (excessive fission, impaired fusion, reduced transport) appear early, before plaque/tangle pathology in some models, and are proposed to drive bioenergetic failure and oxidative damage. Human AD brain and fibroblasts show altered mitochondrial morphology and dynamics, but postmortem findings cannot establish whether fission is a cause or consequence. (e.g., PMID: 16828555; PMID: 19121379)\n- **Tau/neurodegeneration link:** Tau pathology is associated with mitochondrial fragmentation and impaired axonal transport; tau can interact with Drp1 or affect its localization. This connects fission to the tau hypothesis, but causality in humans remains uncertain. (e.g., PMID: 23524341)\n- **Oxidative stress hypothesis:** Fission can increase reactive oxygen species and vice versa, creating a feed-forward cycle. This is largely mechanistic/model-based evidence. (e.g., PMID: 20080728)\n\n**Limitations and uncertainty**\n\n- Most molecular evidence is from **cell culture and transgenic mouse models**; human data are mainly **observational** (postmortem tissue, patient-derived fibroblasts), so **association ≠ causation**.\n- Drp1 changes are not universally replicated across all AD models or stages; fission may be an early compensatory or late degenerative event.\n- The cited PMIDs are provided from existing knowledge; I cannot verify full bibliographic details in this session, so they should be checked before formal citation."
