      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** Evidence on mitochondrial biogenesis in AD is directionally consistent—most AD-relevant studies report *impaired/reduced* biogenesis—but it is derived largely from cell and transgenic animal models, with limited human data, so consistency is moderate rather than definitive.\n\n**Supporting points**\n- Multiple AD-model studies link amyloid/tau pathology to reduced biogenesis. Overexpression of AD-associated mutant APP caused abnormalities of mitochondrial biogenesis and mitophagy (PMID 33080054). In APPswe M17 cells, impaired biogenesis was rescued by PGC-1α overexpression and worsened by PGC-1α knockdown, implicating reduced PGC-1α-driven biogenesis in amyloid toxicity (PMID 22077634).\n- APP/PS1 transgenic mice show altered respiratory function, mitochondrial biogenesis, and mitophagy (PMID 29715546). In tau models, crossing Drp1(+/−) with P301L tau mice enhanced mitochondrial biogenesis and synaptic activity, suggesting tau-related fission excess suppresses biogenesis (PMID 37251647); partial DRP1 deletion also elevated biogenesis against APP- and tau-induced toxicity (PMID 32679689).\n- Mechanistic regulators converge on the AMPK–SIRT1–PGC-1α axis: AMPK regulates biogenesis and is dysregulated early in AD (PMID 35723096); SIRT1 deacetylates PGC-1α to increase biogenesis (PMID 34836000; PMID 30081787); SIRT1/SIRT3 reduce TFAM acetylation, increasing biogenesis (PMID 31597307).\n- Therapeutic/animal evidence supports the same direction: TrkB agonist R13 promoted biogenesis (PGC-1α, NRF1, TFAM) in 5xFAD and SOD1-G93A mice (PMID 37533767).\n\n**Limitations and interpretation**\n- Most records are from AD mouse models or cell lines; direct human AD evidence for biogenesis is sparse in these records, so human relevance remains uncertain.\n- Several supporting records concern non-AD conditions (ALS, Huntington, Parkinson, AMD, cancer), so they inform mechanism but not AD-specific consistency.\n- Findings are associative/mechanistic; causality in human AD is not established. Some contexts show *compensatory increases* in biogenesis (e.g., PMID 36901848), indicating direction can vary by model and stage.\n- Overall: consistent preclinical signal of reduced biogenesis in AD, but insufficient human validation and some conflicting/compensatory findings warrant cautious interpretation."
