      "id": "R-93F375FC",
      "question_id": "H1",
      "question_en": "What representative gene alteration–phenotype relationships are relevant to the amyloid hypothesis of AD?",
      "question_kind": "hypothesis",
      "reference_points": {
        "intro_en": "Provide representative gene alteration–phenotype relationships relevant to amyloid processing, accumulation or clearance.",
        "points": [
          {
            "en": "APP duplication links increased gene dosage to abundant parenchymal/vascular Aβ deposition, cerebral amyloid angiopathy and familial early-onset AD. [R01](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16369530/)"
          },
          {
            "en": "Pathogenic APP and PSEN1/PSEN2 variants link altered APP processing to increased longer Aβ species in the studied mutation carriers and cells. [R34](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8705854/)"
          },
          {
            "en": "APP A673T provides a protective counterexample: reduced amyloidogenic peptide formation in vitro accompanies lower AD risk in human genetic data. [R04](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22801501/)"
          },
          {
            "en": "APOE isoforms link Aβ clearance to accumulation: apoE4-associated slower clearance in amyloid-model mice is distinct from a universal increase in Aβ synthesis. [R05](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21715678/)"
          }
        ],
        "boundary_en": "Credit correctly linked alteration–phenotype examples, not gene names alone. The listed examples are not an exclusive checklist. Distinguish disease-causing mutations from risk alleles and protective variants; do not claim every PSEN mutation raises total Aβ or that APOE4 is deterministic. Equivalent well-supported examples may replace these."
      },
      "answer_blocks": [
        {
          "en": "**Direct answer:** The amyloid hypothesis is most strongly supported by rare, highly penetrant autosomal-dominant mutations in APP, PSEN1, and PSEN2 that increase Aβ42 production or aggregation and cause early-onset familial AD. APOE ε4 is a major risk-modifying allele, while APP duplications and some APP mutations cause AD or cerebral amyloid angiopathy. These gene–phenotype relationships show that altered amyloid precursor protein (APP) processing can be sufficient to cause AD, but they do not prove that amyloid is the sole or initiating cause in all late-onset AD."
        },
        {
          "en": "**Key relationships**"
        },
        {
          "en": "- **APP missense mutations (e.g., Swedish, Indiana, London):** cause early-onset familial AD; many increase total Aβ or the Aβ42/Aβ40 ratio. The Swedish mutation increases β-secretase cleavage (PMID: 2892399). The Indiana and London mutations alter γ-secretase cleavage to favor Aβ42 (PMID: 1302033; PMID: 7651531)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **APP duplication:** causes early-onset AD with cerebral amyloid angiopathy, indicating that increased APP/Aβ dose is pathogenic (PMID: 11524469; PMID: 11689489)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **PSEN1 mutations:** the most common cause of early-onset familial AD; many increase Aβ42 production, supporting a gain-of-function effect on γ-secretase processing (PMID: 8634711; PMID: 8906788)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **PSEN2 mutations:** rare causes of familial AD, with similar but often less penetrant effects on Aβ42 (PMID: 8986753; PMID: 10071055)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **APOE ε4:** increases risk and lowers age at onset in late-onset AD; ε2 is relatively protective. This is a risk association, not a Mendelian cause (PMID: 8346443; PMID: 26101835)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **TREM2 variants:** alter microglial function and increase AD risk, but act outside the core amyloidogenic mutations and indicate immune/inflammatory contributions (PMID: 23150908; PMID: 23150934)."
        },
        {
          "en": "**Interpretation and limitations**"
        },
        {
          "en": "Human genetic evidence is strongest for causation for APP/PSEN mutations and APP duplication. APOE and TREM2 are susceptibility factors, not sufficient causes. Cell and animal models show that Aβ accumulation can produce synaptic and cognitive deficits, but species differences and failure of anti-amyloid therapies to fully halt progression indicate that amyloid is likely necessary in familial forms but not sufficient to explain all AD. The relationship between amyloid plaques and cognitive decline is imperfect; tau pathology and neuroinflammation also contribute. Thus, the amyloid hypothesis is well supported as a causal pathway in rare familial AD, but its role in sporadic late-onset AD remains incompletely resolved."
        }
      ],
