      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** The AD-Alterome evidence packet links the neuroinflammation hypothesis to several immune signaling pathways, but most links are genetic associations or model-based mechanisms rather than proven causal human pathways. Documented pathways include: adaptive antigen presentation (HLA-DRB1), innate myeloid/efferocytosis signaling (TREM2, ABCA7, PLCG2, ABI3), TLR4–NF-κB/MAPK inflammatory signaling, JAK/STAT3 cytokine signaling, CCR7-dependent meningeal lymphatic immune function, and TGF-β/TβRII signaling.\n\n**Supporting points:**\n\n- **Adaptive immunity / antigen presentation:** SNPs in HLA-DRB1, \"critical in facilitating antigen presentation to T cells,\" are present in late-onset AD patients (PMID 36517712). This is a human genetic association, not proof of causation.\n- **Innate myeloid signaling and efferocytosis:** Rare AD risk variants occur in myeloid-specific genes TREM2, ABCA7, PLCG2, and ABI3, with roles in phagocytic clearance of apoptotic cells and debris by microglia (PMID 36907103). TREM2 variants are also associated with senile dementia and increased AD risk (PMID 37274187). ABCA7 mutations reportedly increase Aβ production and neuroinflammatory plaques in vivo (PMID 38241287).\n- **TLR4–NF-κB/MAPK:** In APP/PS1 mice, silencing of brain LRP1 induced TLR4-mediated NF-κB and MAPK activation, increased microgliosis/astrogliosis, and raised TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6 (PMID 35401152). This is animal-model mechanistic evidence.\n- **JAK/STAT3:** In an APP/PS1 transgenic mouse model, STAT3 knockout decreased pro-inflammatory cytokine activation, reduced Aβ levels and plaque burden, and improved spatial learning (PMID 32474469). Animal-model evidence.\n- **CCR7 / meningeal lymphatic immune function:** CCR7 knockout increased AD-related pathology (Aβ deposition, vascular damage, microglial activation) and worsened cognition in a familial AD animal model (PMID 37081555).\n- **TGF-β/TβRII:** Dysfunction of the TGF-β/TβRII axis in the AD brain may accelerate Aβ deposition and neurodegeneration (PMID 37600517); this card was flagged needs_review, so treat as suggestive.\n- **Other reported links:** PI3K/Akt and Wnt signaling appear in the packet, but their sentences concern neuronal/neural-stem-cell effects or AD-mouse apoptosis rather than immune signaling per se (PMIDs 37600517, 37735992). EGFR signaling is mentioned with AD among several diseases (PMID 26869525), and the sentence does not establish an AD-specific immune mechanism.\n\n**Limitations:** These records are sentence-level retrieval candidates, not expert-verified causal claims. Most immune-pathway links are genetic associations (HLA-DRB1, TREM2, ABCA7, ABI3) or animal/cell-model mechanisms (STAT3, CCR7, TLR4–NF-κB/MAPK); human causal evidence remains limited. Several cards were flagged needs_review, and the packet performs no external literature search, so coverage is bounded and conflicting findings may exist."
