      "id": "R-8375F8DA",
      "question_id": "H4",
      "question_en": "What molecular evidence links the oxidative stress hypothesis of AD to mitochondrial dysfunction?",
      "question_kind": "hypothesis",
      "reference_points": {
        "intro_en": "Connect molecular oxidative injury or antioxidant disruption to mitochondrial damage or impaired function.",
        "points": [
          {
            "en": "Human AD brain shows increased oxidized DNA bases, including mitochondrial DNA damage, providing tissue-level evidence of oxidative injury affecting mitochondria. [R24](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15857398/)"
          },
          {
            "en": "In APP/PS1 knock-in mice, mitochondrial MnSOD is nitrated and its activity decreases despite unchanged protein abundance, alongside reduced mitochondrial respiration. [R40](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16651627/)"
          },
          {
            "en": "Female 3xTg-AD mice link decreased respiration and pyruvate dehydrogenase function with increased hydrogen peroxide and lipid peroxidation; early timing is model-specific. [R41](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19667196/)"
          },
          {
            "en": "Mitochondrial SOD2 overexpression lowers superoxide and mitigates memory deficits and plaque deposition in Tg2576 mice, adding intervention-based support. [R42](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19666610/)"
          }
        ],
        "boundary_en": "Require molecular evidence connecting oxidative injury to mitochondria, not simply a list of ROS markers. Distinguish protein abundance from activity, association from intervention, and human tissue from mouse models. Other well-supported mitochondrial oxidative mechanisms may substitute for these examples; neither all four examples nor exact effect sizes are required."
      },
      "answer_blocks": [
        {
          "en": "**Direct answer:** The AD-Alterome evidence links oxidative stress to mitochondrial dysfunction mainly through shared pathological cascades and gene-level mechanisms, but most records are associative or model-based rather than causal human proof."
        },
        {
          "en": "**Supporting points**"
        },
        {
          "en": "- Mitochondrial dysfunction in AD is described as arising alongside Aβ accumulation, tau tangles, and oxidative stress, indicating these processes co-occur in disease pathogenesis (PMID 35950245)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- A mechanistic hypothesis: impaired AD-associated mutant TOMM40 (F113L, F131L) is proposed to cause mitochondrial Aβ accumulation, mitochondrial malfunction, and microglial oxidative stress, leading to NLRP3 inflammasome activation and neuronal death (PMID 36835494). This is explicitly a hypothesis, not demonstrated causation."
        },
        {
          "en": "- In an APP mutant transgenic mouse model, increased mitochondrial gene expression and oxidative damage were reported, supporting a mitochondria–oxidative damage link in an animal model (PMID 25531259)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- In non-AD-specific models, LDL receptor knockout mice showed brain oxidative stress together with mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 38056504); TMEM97 knockout in RPE cells exacerbated oxidative stress and destabilized mitochondria and lysosomes, with organelle dysfunction elevating ROS (PMID 34245862). These are not AD experiments."
        },
        {
          "en": "- Human postmortem AD brain samples with the common TREM2 variant showed elevated oxidative-stress-related and lipid-metabolism genes alongside impaired myelination (PMID 36605616), an association in human tissue."
        },
        {
          "en": "- Genetic/oxidative links: trisomy 21 SOD1 overexpression increases oxidative stress in Down syndrome, which carries high AD risk (PMID 31582732); TNIP1 variants may affect the oxidative stress response via altered GPX3 (PMID 38809917); the UNC5C T835M AD-associated mutation leads to neurodegeneration involving oxidative stress in aged mice (PMID 40468412)."
        },
        {
          "en": "**Limitations**"
        },
        {
          "en": "- Most evidence is correlative or from cell/animal models; direct human causal evidence linking oxidative stress to mitochondrial dysfunction in AD is limited."
        },
        {
          "en": "- Several records (e.g., PMID 38056504, 34245862, 39877004) lack explicit AD context; database AD labels do not establish an AD experiment."
        },
        {
          "en": "- The TOMM40 mechanism is stated as a hypothesis, not confirmed."
        },
        {
          "en": "- This synthesis is based on a bounded AD-Alterome candidate pool with lexical screening only; it does not establish full literature coverage or expert-verified causality."
        }
      ],
