      "answer_blocks": [
        {
          "en": "**Direct answer:** The oxidative stress hypothesis and mitochondrial dysfunction in AD are linked by molecular evidence showing that amyloid-β (Aβ) and hyperphosphorylated tau impair mitochondrial electron transport, increase reactive oxygen species (ROS), and damage mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA), while oxidative damage in turn amplifies Aβ production and tau pathology. This creates a feed-forward cycle, but most mechanistic evidence comes from cell and animal models; human data are largely correlative."
        },
        {
          "en": "**Supporting points:**"
        },
        {
          "en": "- **Aβ inhibits mitochondrial function and raises ROS.** Aβ accumulates in mitochondria, binds Aβ-binding alcohol dehydrogenase (ABAD), and impairs complexes III and IV, increasing hydrogen peroxide and oxidative damage. Human AD brain and transgenic mouse studies support this (PMID: 11140685; PMID: 14645216)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **Mitochondrial DNA and oxidative markers are altered in AD.** Human AD brains show increased 8-hydroxy-2′-deoxyguanosine (8-OHdG) and mtDNA mutations/deletions, especially in vulnerable regions, consistent with mitochondrial ROS damage (PMID: 10767347; PMID: 11750198)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **Tau affects mitochondrial transport and ROS.** Hyperphosphorylated tau disrupts microtubule-based mitochondrial transport and promotes mitochondrial fragmentation and oxidative stress in cell and mouse models (PMID: 19136967; PMID: 25135642)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **Oxidative stress promotes Aβ and tau pathology.** ROS can increase β-secretase (BACE1) activity and Aβ production, and oxidatively modified proteins promote tau aggregation, forming a vicious cycle (PMID: 15208265; PMID: 15546861)."
        },
        {
          "en": "- **Mitochondrial dysfunction precedes amyloid plaques in some models.** In transgenic mice, mitochondrial abnormalities and oxidative stress appear before plaque deposition, suggesting an early causal role, though species differences limit extrapolation (PMID: 14645216)."
        },
        {
          "en": "**Limitations and uncertainty:** Human evidence is mostly postmortem and cross-sectional, so causation is not established. Antioxidant clinical trials have generally failed to slow AD, weakening a simple oxidative-stress causal model. Some findings conflict on whether oxidative damage is primary or secondary. The strongest molecular link is a bidirectional Aβ/tau–mitochondria–ROS cycle, but its quantitative contribution to human AD progression remains uncertain."
        }
      ],
