      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** The oxidative-stress and mitochondrial hypotheses of AD converge on shared molecular nodes—mutant AD genes and mitochondrial quality-control proteins that simultaneously raise reactive oxygen species (ROS) and impair mitochondrial function. Most evidence is associative or from cell/animal models; direct human causal proof is limited.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- **PINK1 (mitophagy/ROS):** Loss or knockdown of PINK1 produces excessive ROS together with increased NF-κB subunits, BACE1, γ-secretase and Aβ accumulation—linking failed mitochondrial clearance to oxidative damage and amyloidogenic processing (PMID 40320714). PINK1 and LRRK2 mutations perturb mitochondrial calcium handling, sensitizing neurons to excitatory injury (PMID 40191272).\n- **PSEN1 (familial AD):** PSEN1 mutations augment oxidative stress and mitochondrial malfunction, aggravating neuronal damage (PMID 40511089). PSEN1 M139I cells show greater ER-stress/apoptosis susceptibility under H₂O₂ (PMID 41704171). APOE4/PSEN1 patient fibroblasts display mitochondrial network fragmentation, superoxide anion elevation and lysosomal/autophagic changes (PMID 37455931).\n- **TOMM40 (mitochondrial import):** Mutant TOMM40 (F113L/F131L) impaired mitochondrial function and activated microglia in BV2 cells (PMID 41692154).\n- **MBLAC1:** Deletion disrupted mitochondrial redox homeostasis in peripheral tissues, partly reversed by restoring Cu(I)-dependent metabolism (PMID 41548934).\n- **Tau/mtDNA:** Tau mutants reduced the mitophagy receptor FKBP8 under paraquat-induced oxidative stress (PMID 39752365); accumulated mtDNA mutations worsened neurodegeneration in an AD mouse model (PMID 37607925).\n\n**Interpretation and limitations**\n\nThese findings indicate a bidirectional, self-reinforcing loop: oxidative stress impairs mitochondria, and mitochondrial dysfunction amplifies ROS and Aβ/tau pathology. However, most records derive from cell lines or transgenic mice (e.g., BV2 microglia, patient fibroblasts, POLG mice), so they demonstrate mechanism plausibility rather than causation in humans. Several retrieved records (e.g., MTHFR, DBH, LPIN1) concern oxidative-stress-adjacent pathways without directly measuring mitochondrial function, and some lack stable identifiers or full citation details, so they cannot be reliably cited here. No single human study in this set establishes that oxidative stress causes mitochondrial dysfunction in AD; the relationship remains correlative and model-dependent."
