      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** Available molecular alteration–phenotype evidence supports the mitochondrial autophagy hypothesis of AD only partially and mostly indirectly. It consistently links AD-related genes and risk variants to mitochondrial dysfunction, but few records demonstrate altered mitophagy flux itself, and most findings are from cell or animal models rather than human tissue.\n\n**Supporting points**\n- **Genetic risk factors converge on mitochondrial integrity.** APOE4 and TREM2 variants are reported to compromise mitochondrial integrity and lipid metabolism (PMID 42071158); ABCA7 deletion disrupts lipid and mitochondrial homeostasis (PMID 41912785); MS4A4A deletion impairs microglial phagocytosis with disrupted mitochondrial metabolic fitness (PMID 40349168); PLCγ2-P522R regulates mitochondrial fatty-acid oxidation (PMID 40038760).\n- **Familial AD mutations impair mitochondria.** PSEN1 mutations increase oxidative stress and mitochondrial malfunction (PMID 40511089); PS1 mutations impair mitochondrial function (PMID 41800250); PS1-M146L enhances IP3R-mediated permeability transition pore opening with loss of membrane potential and ATP (PMID 33271984).\n- **APP/tau pathways affect mitochondrial dynamics and mitophagy.** APPswe alters mitochondrial dynamics, biogenesis and protein import (PMID 34880748) and causes fragmentation reversed by BACE inhibition (PMID 34220427); mitochondrial APP/Aβ40 inhibits hyperactive mitophagy in aging T cells (PMID 40773679); tau mutants reduce the mitophagy receptor FKBP8 (PMID 39752365); P301L tau induces fragmentation, ROS and ATP loss (PMID 35787292).\n- **Direct mitophagy/autophagy links exist but are sparse.** PINK1, LRRK2 and AD-linked proteins perturb mitochondrial calcium handling (PMID 40191272); VPS35 deletion promotes mitochondrial fragmentation (PMID 41751560); mtRNA leakage occurs in AD/FTD mutant neurons (PMID 40570368).\n\n**Limitations and interpretation**\nMost records are associative, from mouse or cell models, and concern general mitochondrial dysfunction rather than measured autophagic clearance; several are oncology or non-AD contexts (e.g., PMID 40773679, 41727571). Human post-mortem or biomarker evidence for impaired mitophagy is largely absent from these records. Causation is therefore not established; the hypothesis is plausible and mechanistically supported but not yet confirmed."
