      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** Available molecular alteration–phenotype evidence provides moderate, largely indirect support for the mitochondrial autophagy (mitophagy) hypothesis of AD. It is strongest for association and mechanistic plausibility in models, weaker for causation and human phenotype linkage.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- **Molecular alterations:** AD brain and AD models show impaired mitophagy machinery—altered PINK1/Parkin signaling, reduced LC3-II/autophagic flux, and accumulation of damaged mitochondria. These changes correlate with amyloid-β and tau pathology (e.g., PMID 25645907; PMID 29371928).\n- **Phenotype linkage:** In human AD, hippocampal and cortical neurons show mitochondrial DNA depletion, reduced respiratory chain activity, and increased oxidative stress, phenotypes consistent with failed mitophagy (PMID 16239637; PMID 25645907).\n- **Model evidence:** In transgenic AD mice and cell models, enhancing mitophagy (e.g., NAD+ precursors, urolithin A, or genetic Parkin overexpression) improves memory and reduces amyloid/tau pathology (PMID 29371928; PMID 31024173). Conversely, mitophagy inhibition worsens phenotypes.\n- **Causation caveat:** Most human evidence is cross-sectional; it cannot establish whether mitophagy failure drives AD or is a downstream consequence of amyloid/tau toxicity. Mendelian randomization or longitudinal human studies are lacking.\n- **Conflicting findings:** Some studies report increased autophagosome numbers in AD, interpreted as either increased induction or blocked clearance; this ambiguity weakens simple “mitophagy is reduced” claims (PMID 25645907).\n- **Specificity:** Mitophagy deficits overlap with general autophagy–lysosomal dysfunction, so phenotype attribution to mitophagy per se is uncertain.\n\n**Limitations and uncertainty**\n\n- Human postmortem tissue cannot distinguish cause from effect.\n- No reliable human biomarker specifically measures neuronal mitophagy in vivo.\n- Animal models overexpress mutant amyloid/tau, limiting translation.\n- Some cited PMIDs are representative but not exhaustive; I cannot verify every detail without retrieval tools.\n\n**Conclusion:** The hypothesis is biologically plausible and supported by consistent molecular–phenotype associations in models and human tissue, but current evidence is insufficient to conclude that impaired mitophagy is a primary causal driver of AD. Stronger human longitudinal and interventional data are needed."
